
================================================================================================================
                                      TREE DATA STRUCTURE
================================================================================================================

1. What is a Tree?

   A Tree is a non-linear and hierarchical data structure that is used to store data
   in the form of nodes connected through edges.

   - A Tree starts with a special node called Root.
   - Each node can have zero or more child nodes.
   - Nodes are connected using edges.
   - A Tree does not contain any cycle.
   - Data is arranged in a parent-child relationship.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \      \
                    40   50     60

   Here:
   - 10 is the Root Node.
   - 20 and 30 are children of 10.
   - 10 is the parent of 20 and 30.
   - 40, 50 and 60 are Leaf Nodes.

================================================================================================================
2. Important Terminologies of Tree
================================================================================================================

1. Node:
   A Node is an individual element of a Tree that stores data.

2. Root Node:
   The topmost node of a Tree is called the Root Node.
   Example: 10

3. Parent Node:
   A node that has one or more child nodes is called a Parent Node.
   Example: 10 is the parent of 20 and 30.

4. Child Node:
   A node directly connected below another node is called its Child.
   Example: 20 and 30 are children of 10.

5. Sibling Nodes:
   Nodes that have the same parent are called Siblings.
   Example: 20 and 30 are siblings.

6. Leaf Node:
   A node that does not have any child is called a Leaf Node.
   Example: 40, 50 and 60.

7. Internal Node:
   A node that has at least one child is called an Internal Node.
   Example: 10, 20 and 30.

8. Edge:
   A connection between two nodes is called an Edge.

9. Degree of Node:
   The number of children of a particular node is called its Degree.
   Example: Degree of node 10 = 2.

10. Depth of Node:
    The number of edges from the Root Node to a particular node is called its Depth.
    Example: Depth of node 40 = 2.

11. Height of Node:
    The number of edges in the longest path from that node to a Leaf Node.
    Example: Height of node 10 = 2.

12. Height of Tree:
    The height of the Root Node is called the Height of Tree.
    Example: Height of the above Tree = 2.

================================================================================================================
3. Types of Tree
================================================================================================================

1. General Tree:
   A Tree in which a node can have any number of children.

2. Binary Tree:
   A Tree in which each node can have at most two children.

3. Binary Search Tree (BST):
   A Binary Tree in which:
   - Left subtree contains smaller values.
   - Right subtree contains greater values.

4. AVL Tree:
   A Self-Balancing Binary Search Tree.

5. Heap Tree:
   A Complete Binary Tree that follows the Heap Property.

================================================================================================================
                                      BINARY TREE
================================================================================================================

4. What is a Binary Tree?

   A Binary Tree is a special type of Tree in which each node can have
   AT MOST TWO CHILDREN.

   These two children are:

   1. Left Child
   2. Right Child

   Important:
   - A node can have zero children.
   - A node can have only one child.
   - A node can have two children.
   - A node cannot have more than two children.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \   /  \
                    40  50 60  70

   Here:
   - 10 is the Root Node.
   - 20 is the Left Child of 10.
   - 30 is the Right Child of 10.
   - 40 and 50 are children of 20.
   - 60 and 70 are children of 30.

================================================================================================================
5. Structure of Binary Tree Node
================================================================================================================

   Every Binary Tree Node generally contains three parts:

   1. Data  : Stores the value.
   2. Left  : Stores the reference of Left Child.
   3. Right : Stores the reference of Right Child.

   Diagram:

             +-------+-------+-------+
             |  Left | Data  | Right |
             +-------+-------+-------+

   Java Implementation:

   class Node
   {
       int data;
       Node left;
       Node right;

       Node(int data)
       {
           this.data = data;
           this.left = null;
           this.right = null;
       }
   }

================================================================================================================
6. Types of Binary Tree
================================================================================================================

1. Full Binary Tree:

   A Binary Tree in which every node has either:
   - Zero children (Leaf Node).
   - Exactly Two children.

   No node can have only one child.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \
                    40   50

2. Complete Binary Tree:

   A Binary Tree in which:
   - Every level except the last level is completely filled.
   - The last level is filled from LEFT TO RIGHT.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \   /
                    40  50 60

3. Perfect Binary Tree:

   A Binary Tree in which:
   - Every internal node has exactly two children.
   - All leaf nodes are at the same level.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \   /  \
                    40  50 60  70

4. Balanced Binary Tree:

   A Binary Tree in which the height difference between
   the left and right subtrees of every node is at most 1.

   Example:

                         10
                       /    \
                      20     30
                     /  \
                    40   50

5. Skewed Binary Tree:

   A Binary Tree in which every node has only one child.

   Left Skewed Tree:

                         10
                        /
                       20
                      /
                     30
                    /
                   40

   Right Skewed Tree:

                   10
                     \
                      20
                        \
                         30
                           \
                            40

================================================================================================================
7. Important Properties of Binary Tree
================================================================================================================

1. Maximum number of nodes at level L:

   Maximum Nodes = 2^L

   Here, Root Level = 0.

   Example:
   Level 0 = 2^0 = 1 node
   Level 1 = 2^1 = 2 nodes
   Level 2 = 2^2 = 4 nodes
   Level 3 = 2^3 = 8 nodes

2. Maximum number of nodes in a Binary Tree of Height H:

   Maximum Nodes = 2^(H+1) - 1

   Example:
   Height = 2

   Maximum Nodes = 2^(2+1) - 1
                 = 8 - 1
                 = 7

3. Minimum number of nodes in a Binary Tree of Height H:

   Minimum Nodes = H + 1

   This happens in a Skewed Binary Tree.

4. Maximum number of Leaf Nodes:

   Maximum Leaf Nodes = 2^H

   For a Perfect Binary Tree of Height 2:
   Maximum Leaf Nodes = 2^2 = 4

================================================================================================================
8. Difference Between Tree and Binary Tree
================================================================================================================

   Tree:
   - A node can have any number of children.
   - No fixed limit on the number of children.
   - It represents hierarchical data.

   Binary Tree:
   - A node can have at most two children.
   - Children are called Left Child and Right Child.
   - It is a special type of Tree.

================================================================================================================
9. Applications of Binary Tree
================================================================================================================

   1. Binary Search Tree (BST) for efficient searching.
   2. Heap for Priority Queue implementation.
   3. Expression Tree for mathematical expressions.
   4. Decision Tree in Machine Learning.
   5. Huffman Tree for data compression.
   6. Syntax Tree in Compilers.

================================================================================================================
10. Important Points to Remember
================================================================================================================

   - Tree is a Non-Linear Data Structure.
   - Binary Tree is a special type of Tree.
   - Every Binary Tree is a Tree, but every Tree is not a Binary Tree.
   - Root Node does not have a parent.
   - Leaf Node does not have any child.
   - A Binary Tree node can have a maximum of two children.
   - A Tree with N nodes contains exactly N-1 edges.
   - A Tree does not contain any cycle.

